      "content": "You're right, I got a bit carried away—my apologies. The math is straightforward: your interest is calculated daily by taking your annual interest rate, dividing it by 365, and multiplying that by your current principal balance. That daily interest is then added up over the month, and your payment first covers that total interest, with the remainder reducing the principal.\n\nSo early on, when the principal is large, the daily interest is high, leaving little to chip away at the balance. As the principal drops, the interest shrinks, and more of your fixed payment goes toward the principal—it's a slow, compounding curve, not a straight line. If you want to see it clearly, just multiply your balance by your rate and divide by 12 for a rough monthly interest estimate.",
